    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. I really want to lean into that \"visceral\" aspect though—make the reader feel the divide immediately. When you talk about the Low-Tiers having \"toughened skin\" or a \"ruder\" way of speaking, could you get more specific with the actual physical descriptions? Like, what does their skin look like, their hair, or their facial features compared to the elites? And for the culture, what kind of \"raw\" habits or vices would they have that would make the High-Tiers look down on them as being less civilized or more primal?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the Low-Tier's context, the skin could have a leathery texture, grimed with soot and the dust of sub-standard living. Their hair would be coarse, either unkempt or tightly knotted away from their faces to work. They may have more pronounced, rugged facial structures, with a deeper set of eyes filled with hidden yearning or cunning, compared to the High-Tier's brighter, often hollow gaze, maybe blighted by artificial enhancements or wholly radiant from their untouched, clear-boned features.\n\nAs for habits, the Low-Tier population may indulge in unsanctioned bartering and black-market trades, living on the fringes and leaving no record of their transactions. Their vices could include substance abuse made from illegal or forbidden contraband, kept hidden from the High-Tiers' prying eyes. They also apply inventive survival strategies, using scrap metal and discarded technology to keep themselves warm or to steal needed resources, something utterly alien to the High-Tier lifestyle.\n\nDespite the oppression, within their communities, they manage to foster a fierce loyalty to one another, passing on skills not only of physical prowess but also of subterfuge and survival against the oppressive high society. This culture includes old-breaking tricks, underground",
      "source": "target",
